The {{nihongo|'''Taiju War'''|大樹の乱|Taiju no Ran}}, also known as the {{nihongo|Ten-Year Chaos|十年動乱|Jūnen Dōran}}, was a transformative civil war in the northern archipelago of Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]) that spanned from 1467 to 1477. Rooted in the {{nihongo|Triple Succession Crisis|三種継承|Sanshu Keisō}}, the conflict saw the complete disintegration of the [[Minato Shogunate]]'s administrative power. The war is characterized by the total destruction of the ancient capital of Mito-kyō and the emergence of independent provincial warlords, marking the official beginning of the [[Sengoku period]].

== Background and Path to Hostilities ==

By the Bunshō era (1466), the Shogunal succession had become a three-way deadlock between the powerful {{nihongo|shugo|守護}} (military governors) supporting different branches of the Minato bloodline. The Ise Faction, led by Kaneitsuka Yoshimitsu, supported Murakami Yoshihide. The Saitama Faction, led by Muromachi Katsuyo, supported Katsuura Masayoshi. The Seto Faction, led by the monk-general Sugi Masayoshi, supported the Shogun's brother, Yoshimasa.

The conflict turned violent when Katsuura Masayoshi was assassinated by Kaneitsuka agents at the Gion-ji temple in Mito-kyō. In retaliation, Muromachi forces launched a preemptive strike against the Kaneitsuka mansion, executing the Ise heir and igniting a city-wide fire that burned for seven days. This initial massacre, known as the {{nihongo|Blood of Gion-ji|祇園寺の血潮|Gion-ji no Chishio}}, signaled the end of diplomatic negotiations and the start of a total war for survival.

== Course of the Conflict ==

The opening years of the conflict were defined by the {{nihongo|Kamo-gawa Escalation|賀茂川激化|Kamo-gawa Gekika}} (1467–1468). Following the initial fires in Mito-kyō, the war settled into a street-to-street struggle. The Western Army, entrenched in the Kamigyo district, utilized the Kamo-gawa river as a natural moat. In late 1467, Kaneitsuka Yoshimitsu attempted the {{nihongo|Assault on the Silk Gates|絹門の戦い|Kinu-mon no Tatakai}}. His forces were repulsed by Tachibana Josetsu, who employed specialized pike-walls ({{nihongo|nagae-yari|長柄槍}}) to pin the Ise cavalry against the burning merchant stalls. Over 5,000 samurai fell in the river, clogging the waterways and leading to the {{nihongo|Year of the Red Mist|紅霧の年|Kōmu no Toshi}}, a localized plague caused by unburied corpses in the stagnant waters of the capital.

In mid-1468, the {{nihongo|Skirmish at Rokujō-kawara|六条河原の合戦|Rokujō-kawara no Kassen}} broke out when Eastern foraging parties were ambushed near the riverbanks. The engagement was brief but savage, resulting in the death of the Muromachi captain Tachibana Nobutada. Nobutada was cornered against a temple wall by Shibata {{nihongo|ashigaru|足軽}}; he was skewered through the torso by three long spears simultaneously. The force of the thrusts was so great that the soldiers heaved their spear-butts downward, lifting Nobutada's dying body high into the air as a gruesome signal of victory.

Shortly after, the fighting escalated at the {{nihongo|Battle of Shiroi-zaka|白井坂の戦い|Shiroi-zaka no Tatakai}}. Seeking to relieve the starving Western garrison, Muromachi Katsuyo attempted a breakout through the Shiroi hills. He was met by the rising military prodigy Shibata Kanegetsu, who deployed a staggered pike formation that decimated the Muromachi cavalry. During the retreat, the Western Army burned the venerable Shōhei Temple to slow the pursuit, killing over 400 unarmed monks and destroying centuries of Sanese scripture.

The conflict reached its most apocalyptic point in 1469 at the {{nihongo|Battle of Komaki-hara|小牧原の戦い|Komaki-hara no Tatakai}}. Seeking to break the urban stalemate, the Eastern Army retreated to the Komaki-hara plains to draw the Western Army out. Shibata Kanegetsu took command of the vanguard and utilized the {{nihongo|Twelve Lanterns|十二灯|Jūnishi-tō}} strategy, using wind-currents to send fire-balloons into the Muromachi baggage train. The resulting explosion of black powder—the first major use of Yanbaru-traded explosives—shattered the Western morale. During the retreat, the Western Army burned the Hōrin-ji library to the ground, destroying the original Jofun Code scrolls. This act of cultural destruction, known as the {{nihongo|Ash-Fall|灰降|Haifuri}}, permanently erased the legal records of the ancient centralized state.

In 1470, the {{nihongo|Skirmish of the Golden Pavilion|金閣寺の小戦い|Kinkaku-ji no Shou-tatakai}} occurred when a rogue detachment of Eastern ronin attempted to seize the Imperial treasures hidden within the northern outskirts. They were met by the Muromachi "Iron Guard." The fighting resulted in the death of the Ise commander Imazato Yoshiaki. Yoshiaki was caught in a chaotic melee near the pavilion lake; after being unhorsed, he was beheaded on the spot by a Western samurai, who then paraded the severed head on a {{nihongo|naginata|長刀}} to signify the total repulse of the Eastern forces.

By 1471, the war shifted to the vital waterways in the {{nihongo|Battle of Sansuikai|三水会の戦い|Sansuikai no Tatakai}}. The Western Army launched an amphibious assault to reclaim grain routes from the southern provinces, using flat-bottomed barges armored with wet hides. The intervention of Sugi Sea-Monks from the Shoto shrines turned the engagement into a chaotic three-way naval battle. Sugi Masayoshi deployed {{nihongo|tekkōsen|鉄甲船}} precursors, iron-clad barges that utilized primitive fire-pipes to ignite Eastern blockades. The slaughter was immense; contemporary chronicles claim the Mito River ran red for three days.

The {{nihongo|Skirmish of the Katsura Pass|桂峠の合戦|Katsura-tōge no Kassen}} in 1472 focused on the supply lines between Seto and the Kanto. Sugi monks ambushed a massive Kaneitsuka supply train, utilizing the rugged terrain to trap the samurai in a narrow ravine. The Eastern Army lost nearly its entire winter food stock in a single afternoon, leading to the desperate measures seen in the following years.

The Seto-Oura Campaign (1472–1474) saw the war evolve into a grinding stalemate. This period was marked by the massive {{nihongo|Battle of Seikan|青岸の戦い|Seikan no Tatakai}} in 1473, where the Western Army attempted a final, desperate invasion of the Sugi heartland. The battle featured the largest deployment of archers in the war, with over 10,000 men exchanging volleys across the rocky plateaus. The Sugi victory here ensured their continued autonomy and the further depletion of the Muromachi treasury. This period also saw the {{nihongo|Siege of Ise-Kame|伊勢亀城の戦い|Ise-Kame-jō no Tatakai}} (1472), where Shibata Kanegetsu spent eight months suppressing Kaneitsuka deputies. The fall of the fortress signaled the Shibata clan's rise as an independent force, effectively supplanting their former overlords.

The war’s final phase was marked by the {{nihongo|Siege of Mt. Hiei|比叡山包囲|Hiei-zan Hōi}} (1474), where Western Army remnants attempted to seize the temple treasuries. The monks of the Enryaku-ji sect joined the Sugi clan, forming the first Ikkō-ikki leagues. These militant peasants engaged in {{nihongo|Total Defiance|全進抵抗|Zenshin-teikō}}, burning their own crops to starve the approaching armies. This led to the {{nihongo|Ten-Tō Famine|天等飢饉|Ten-Tō Kikin}}, where an estimated 80,000 peasants died in the Kanto corridor, forcing a cessation of hostilities as soldiers deserted to join religious communes. The final violent gasp occurred during the {{nihongo|Raid on Mount Daigo|醍醐山襲撃|Daigo-zan Shūgeki}} (1476), where rogue Western samurai attempted to loot the Silver Pavilion. They were repelled by the Sugi warrior-monks.

== Conclusion and the Natsunaga Protocol ==

The war officially concluded with the {{nihongo|Natsunaga Protocol|夏永の提約|Natsunaga no Teiyaku}} in 1477. By this time, the original purpose of the war—the Shogunal succession—was largely forgotten. The protocol was a recognition of exhaustion rather than a resolution. The surviving warlords, realizing that the capital was now a hollow ruin and the central treasury was empty, simply abandoned Mito-kyō to return to their ancestral lands.

The protocol officially acknowledged the right of provincial lords to govern their lands independently of the Shogunate, provided they paid a nominal tribute to the Emperor. This decentralized peace marked the true beginning of the Sengoku period. The Minato Shogunate continued to exist in name, but its influence was confined to the burned-out shells of the Mito palaces, while the real power lay in the hands of the rising regional daimyo.

== Post-War Era and Gekokujō ==

While the streets of the capital ran with blood, the Shogun and the Emperor famously detached themselves from reality, spending the decade-long war in the secluded Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji. When the Treaty of Natsunaga finally ended the immediate conflict in 1477, the surviving clan armies simply abandoned the depopulated ruins of the capital to return to their ancestral lands, effectively ending the Minato Shogunate's relevance.

The power vacuum left by the ruined capital birthed the phenomenon of {{nihongo|gekokujō|下克上}}, literally translated as the low conquers the high. With the central authority impotent, the traditional prestige of court-appointed governors evaporated. They were ruthlessly overthrown by their own deputies, capable subordinates, or charismatic peasant leaders who rejected hereditary status in favor of meritocratic martial strength.

One of the earliest examples was [[Hōjō Sōun]], a minor rōnin who seized Izu Province in 1493 and established the [[Late Hōjō clan]]. He transformed the Kanto region into a fortress state, ruling independent of any central authority for a century. In every province, the old guard fell; the prestigious Hosokawa clan was supplanted by their vassals, the Miyoshi, and the Toki clan was overthrown by the Saitō, who were in turn destroyed by their own vassal, the peasant-born general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]]. This disintegration of the social hierarchy established a new, violent order that would define the archipelago for the next 150 years.

